content.text:
You understood the selection and edited-copy branches correctly. The missing step is that the loader changes the saved config *before* it reaches those branches. :codex-annotation{index="1"}

When the referenced system or inherited preset is available, the loader calls `update_non_diff_values_to_base_config` on the saved config. For any key **not** listed in `different_settings_to_system`, that function replaces the saved value with the currently installed preset’s value ([Preset.cpp](/Users/kenneth/.codex/worktrees/c375/JusPrin/src/libslic3r/Preset.cpp:2515), [PrintConfig.cpp](/Users/kenneth/.codex/worktrees/c375/JusPrin/src/libslic3r/PrintConfig.cpp:10125)). The later “apply to edited copy” branch applies this *updated* config, not the original saved one ([Preset.cpp](/Users/kenneth/.codex/worktrees/c375/JusPrin/src/libslic3r/Preset.cpp:2605)).

So your conclusion is right **for a setting recorded as different**: its saved value is retained as an edit. But if the setting matched the preset at capture time, it is not recorded as different; a changed installed default can then become the restored effective value. My earlier explanation should have made that distinction explicit.

Not quite. I made “decides which values win” sound like “unlisted values are never read.” That was misleading. :codex-annotation{index="1"}

Orca first loads the **full saved config**. It then replaces an unlisted preset setting *only if* it finds a current base preset that supplies that setting. If it cannot find the base, the saved value remains. Separately, project-specific settings are loaded from the same config and are not governed by the preset override list ([preset load](https://github.com/OrcaSlicer/OrcaSlicer/blob/f3d0b8a553958a10fe94df7d6ca2513d5690e8e3/src/libslic3r/Preset.cpp#L1057-L1086), [project settings load](https://github.com/OrcaSlicer/OrcaSlicer/blob/f3d0b8a553958a10fe94df7d6ca2513d5690e8e3/src/libslic3r/PresetBundle.cpp#L5595-L5609)).

So for an *unlisted* `wall_loops=3`: current base says 2 → restore uses **2**; no resolvable base → restore can keep **3**. Keeping the full values gives the loader that fallback and carries settings that aren’t preset overrides. I can verify those uses in the code; I can’t verify that they were the maintainers’ original reason for choosing this file format.
